From 51b85c8bad57ed4616f72a13a4dcd2155e0f9058 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Turquoise Bay AI <11993289+hughmadden@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 09:57:10 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(dspark): copy-window drafts from the request's own
 history

Agent output often repeats its context: file edits, tool-call arguments that
quote code, paths. When a greedy request's token history (prompt plus
emitted tokens) ends with eight tokens that occurred earlier in that history, the
tokens that followed the most recent earlier occurrence are now that round's
drafts in place of dSpark's. The target verifies them with the same
sample-and-match rule as dSpark drafts, so the output is unchanged; only the
tokens committed per round change. The rule comes from ashhart/TensorFold
(MIT), reimplemented from its description.

Proposer (v41_native_serve/copy_drafts.rs): per request, a std HashMap from
each eight-token window's 64-bit hash to the absolute end position of its
latest occurrence (16-byte entries, a pass-through hasher over the mixed
key), built on the request's first decode round and extended by the tokens
emitted since. The anchor's own window is indexed only in a later round, so
a lookup finds earlier occurrences only, and a candidate is copied only
after its tokens match, since keys can collide. A proposal carries at least
two and at most min(draft limit, remaining budget - 1) tokens, the bound
dSpark's proposals obey. When the source ends fewer tokens before the anchor
than that cap, copying continues from the same distance, so a run or a short
repeat fills the window, as re-matching after each accepted token would. A
copy round that accepts nothing pauses copying for that request for two
rounds. A copy takes the same grammar truncation as dSpark drafts, and one
cut below two tokens is dropped so dSpark drafts the request. Sampled
requests never copy: their draws leave copied text more often, and a copy
that fails costs the request dSpark's drafts for that round. In a mixed soak
with a third of requests at temperature 0.7, copied drafts were accepted 52%
of the time, against 95% on greedy file rewrites.

Scheduler: a copying request is left out of that round's dSpark draft, in
both the single-lane propose and the independent-lane poll_propose, so the
chain drafts only the other requests and a lane whose requests all copy runs
no draft pass. Leaving a request out is safe. Chain graphs are keyed by
request count and width and captured on first use. dSpark's per-request
state is its committed window rows, and the batch commit writes those from
the target's taps for every batch member, drafted or not, as it already does
for a request whose budget allows no draft. DraftRuntime::skip_copied clears
the copying requests' stale confidence and marks a lane with no draft pass
(width 0).

Length policy: a copy bypasses select_lengths and is verified whole; the
policy selects the other requests' lengths as before (select_lengths now
accepts an empty candidate list). The round observation moved into
speculative/policy.rs::observe, unchanged for rounds without copies. A
copying request is observed without confidence, so calibration, per-request
outcomes and recent confidence learn from dSpark drafts only, while its rows
and routes still feed the layer and round fits and its route history. A
round with a copy keeps no prediction, so the time bias does not move. A
draft pass beside a copy covered fewer requests than the lane, so that round
gives no draft-cost sample and its draft time is taken out of the round
total, which leaves the round fit's residual as a round without copies
would.

DS41RT_COPY_DRAFTS=0 disables copy windows (default on; they need dSpark).
The serving worker logs one line at startup saying whether they are on, and
/v1/stats reports copy_drafts {request_rounds, verified_drafts,
accepted_drafts}; dspark_policy's acceptance totals include those rows. With
copies off, or in a lane round in which no request copies, the round makes
the same draft, verification and policy calls, with the same inputs, as
before. README and docs/dspark-bandwidth-policy.md describe the rule and its
policy handling.

Tests: copy_drafts has ten unit tests covering latest-occurrence matching,
the eight-token entry, the two-to-cap length, overlapping sources and runs,
no self-match, a planted key collision, the backoff, the budget cap, merge
order, and the incrementally built index against a brute-force search at
every length of a 3,000-token sequence. policy.rs adds
rounds_without_copies_observe_as_before (identical policy state to the
previous observation) and
copy_rounds_teach_costs_but_never_the_drafters_evidence. Breaking the
confidence exclusion, the draft-time adjustment, the periodic continuation
or the latest-occurrence index each fails a test. No CPU harness drives a
lane round (target passes, draft chains and the request bank need CUDA), so
the exclusion and commit path are covered by reading, not by an integration
test. CPU suite (just test-rust-fast, --locked): 1,920 passed, 8 failed, 142
ignored. The parent gives 1,908 passed with the same 8 failures: five
target-attention tests and one graph-capture test that need Python torch,
one ds41rt-ffi geometry test, and the stale admission test that expects 503
where the queue now answers 429.

